'''December 2025'''

Archaeologists in Jerusalem uncovered a 1,300-year-old lead pendant bearing a seven-branched menorah near the southwestern corner of the Temple Mount, during digs in the Davidson Archaeological Park.<ref name=":11">{{Cite news |last=Tercatin |first=Rossella |date=2025-12-15 |title=1,300-year-old menorah pendant suggests Rome couldn't keep all Jews out of Jerusalem |url=https://www.timesofisrael.com/1300-year-old-menorah-pendant-suggests-romes-ban-on-jews-from-jerusalem-was-a-failure/ |access-date=2025-12-19 |work=The Times of Israel |language=en-US |issn=0040-7909}}</ref><ref name=":14">{{Cite web |date=2025-12-15 |title=1,300-year-old lead Menorah pendant unearthed near Temple Mount |url=https://www.i24news.tv/en/news/israel/archeology/artc-1-300-year-old-lead-menorah-pendant-unearthed-near-temple-mount |access-date=2025-12-19 |website=i24NEWS |language=en}}</ref>

The object is almost pure lead with menorahs on both sides, and is only the second known pendant of its kind, the other held in Baltimore.<ref name=":11" /><ref name=":24">{{Cite web |date=2025-12-19 |title=Archaeologists Found a Rare Pendant Beneath a Temple. It Shouldn't Have Been There. |url=https://www.popularmechanics.com/science/archaeology/a69731799/rare-pendant-beneath-a-temple/ |access-date=2025-12-19 |website=Popular Mechanics |language=en-US}}</ref>

It dates to the Late Byzantine period, when Jews were formally banned from Jerusalem, yet its presence suggests some still visited or maintained ties to the city.<ref name=":11" /><ref name=":14" />

Researchers think it served as a personal amulet symbolizing Jewish identity, not simple jewelry, and its discovery at the foot of the Temple Mount reinforces the enduring Jewish historical and spiritual connection to the site.<ref name=":24" /><ref name=":14" />

