'''Wilson Arch''', also known as '''Wilson's Arch''', is a natural [[sandstone]] arch in southeastern [[Utah]], United States just off [[U.S. Route 191 in Utah|U.S. Route 191]] located in [[San Juan County, Utah|San Juan County]], {{convert|24|mi|km}} south of [[Moab, Utah|Moab]]. It has a span of {{convert|91|ft|m}} and height of {{convert|46|ft|m}}.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.woodchuckimages.com/Utah/Around_Moab/info_hmtl/Info_wilson_arch.html |title=Info - Wilson Arch - Utah |accessdate=17 Jun 2010}}</ref> It is visible from the road to the east where there are turnouts with interpretive signs. The elevation of Wilson Arch is {{convert|6175|ft|m}}.<ref name=gnis/>

According to the sign at the pulloff near the arch:

<blockquote>Wilson Arch was named after Joe Wilson, a local pioneer who had a cabin nearby in [[Dry Valley (San Juan County, Utah)|Dry Valley]].  This formation is known as [[Entrada Sandstone]]. Over time superficial cracks, joints, and folds of these layers were saturated with water. Ice formed in the fissures, melted under extreme desert heat, and winds cleaned out the loose particles. A series of free-standing fins remained. Wind and water attacked these fins until, in some, cementing material gave way and chunks of rock tumbled out.  Many damaged fins collapsed like the one to the right of Wilson Arch. Others, with the right degree of hardness survived despite their missing middles like Wilson Arch.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.teresco.org/pics/fourcorners-20030926-27/27/wilson.html |title=Wilson Arch, Utah|date=September 27, 2003|last=Teresco|first=James D|accessdate=17 Jun 2010}}</ref></blockquote>

